# 5-DOF Robotic Arm

![Robotic Arm](RobotArm.png)

A 5-DOF robotic arm designed, fabricated, and controlled using SolidWorks, 3D-printed components, an Arduino Uno, and five micro servos.

## Project Overview

The goal of this project is to design and fabricate a compact robotic arm capable of controlled movement and manipulation of lightweight objects.

## Features

- 5 degrees of freedom
- 5 micro servo motors
- Arduino Uno control
- SolidWorks CAD design
- 3D-printed PLA components
- Passive shoulder support
- Custom mechanical structure

## Specifications

| Parameter | Value |
|---|---|
| Degrees of Freedom | 5 |
| Arm Length | ~29.5 cm |
| Arm Mass | ~184 g |
| Payload | ~10 g |
| Controller | Arduino Uno |
| Actuators | 5 micro servos |
| Material | PLA |

## Servo Configuration

| Joint | Arduino Pin |
|---|---|
| Base | D9 |
| Shoulder | D10 |
| Elbow | D11 |
| Wrist | D12 |
| Claw | D13 |

## Mechanical Design

The robotic arm was designed in SolidWorks with consideration for servo mounting, mechanical clearance, structural support, and manufacturability using FDM 3D printing.

A passive support was incorporated at the shoulder to reduce the torque experienced by the shoulder servo.

## Electronics

The arm uses an Arduino Uno to control five servo motors.

## Testing

Each servo was initially tested individually before integrating the complete robotic arm.

## Future Improvements

- Increase payload capacity
- Improve shoulder support
- Implement joystick control
- Add inverse kinematics
- Improve claw design
